{
  "format": "loopthing/v0.1",
  "meta": {
    "title": "Origin trace: Steinberger's OpenClaw",
    "source_url": "https://github.com/steipete/OpenClaw",
    "source_kind": "reverse-engineered",
    "note": "Original chats unavailable. Four agents simulate plausible origin DAGs from the published OpenClaw artifact and supplied source notes.",
    "created": "2026-04-29T05:04:00Z"
  },
  "agents": [
    {
      "id": "lock-in",
      "color": "#7F77DD",
      "thesis": "OpenClaw reads as a refusal to let the coding-agent loop belong to one vendor: model, runner, prompts, tools, and billing all need to be swappable."
    },
    {
      "id": "craft",
      "color": "#1D9E75",
      "thesis": "OpenClaw turns Steinberger-style developer ergonomics toward agents: inspect the loop the way you would inspect an IDE, debugger, or build tool."
    },
    {
      "id": "infrastructure",
      "color": "#D85A30",
      "thesis": "OpenClaw treats the agent loop as shared infrastructure: tool routing, shell access, schemas, retries, and safety policy should not be rebuilt badly by every team."
    },
    {
      "id": "canon",
      "color": "#D4537E",
      "thesis": "OpenClaw is the Unix promise applied to AI tools: small, composable, scriptable, model-agnostic pieces instead of a closed monolithic agent."
    }
  ],
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "lock.a",
      "agent": "lock-in",
      "type": "prompt",
      "parent": null,
      "content": "Why should \"the AI that actually does things\" be boxed inside one vendor's model, CLI, billing meter, and hidden prompts?",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:03Z",
      "source_refs": ["OpenClaw site: \"The AI that actually does things.\"", "OpenClaw/OpenAI: \"stay open and independent\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "lock.b",
      "agent": "lock-in",
      "type": "critique",
      "parent": "lock.a",
      "content": "The model is the obvious lock-in. The quieter lock-in is the loop: prompts, tool schemas, retries, provider routing, and the control plane you cannot inspect.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:08Z",
      "source_refs": ["AGENTS.md: \"manifest-first control plane\"", "Tools and Plugins: \"Everything the agent does beyond generating text happens through tools.\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "lock.c.kill",
      "agent": "lock-in",
      "type": "kill",
      "parent": "lock.b",
      "content": "Just proxy the existing closed CLI and rewrite the API calls.",
      "kill_reason": "A proxy treats the invoice as the problem and leaves the loop inside the vendor boundary. The prompts, tool schemas, retry policy, and safety defaults still belong to somebody else.",
      "killed_at": "2026-04-29T05:04:13Z",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:13Z",
      "source_refs": ["Configuration: \"If the file is missing, OpenClaw uses safe defaults.\"", "OpenClaw/OpenAI: \"stay open and independent\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "lock.c",
      "agent": "lock-in",
      "type": "revision",
      "parent": "lock.b",
      "content": "Make the agent loop the open thing: `AGENTS.md` operating memory, skills that teach tools, plugin manifests, safe defaults, and model/provider choice under the operator's rules.",
      "supersedes": "lock.c.kill",
      "why_won": "It moves freedom from the billing layer into the actual loop: prompts, tools, config, providers, and runtime policy become inspectable and forkable.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:19Z",
      "source_refs": ["AGENTS.md", "Skills: \"teach the agent how to use tools.\"", "docs home: \"runs on your hardware, your rules\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "lock.d",
      "agent": "lock-in",
      "type": "synthesis",
      "parent": "lock.c",
      "content": "The open unit is not just the model. It is the whole loop: prompt, runner, tool, policy, channel, and audit trail.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:25Z",
      "source_refs": ["VISION.md: \"Security and safe defaults\"", "SECURITY.md: \"not 'shared multi-tenant bus.'\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "craft.a",
      "agent": "craft",
      "type": "prompt",
      "parent": null,
      "content": "What would serious developer ergonomics demand from an assistant that clears inboxes, sends emails, manages calendars, and checks flights?",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:04Z",
      "source_refs": ["OpenClaw site: \"Clears your inbox, sends emails, manages your calendar, checks you in for flights.\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "craft.b",
      "agent": "craft",
      "type": "critique",
      "parent": "craft.a",
      "content": "Black-box agent theater is the opposite of craft. OpenClaw keeps the loop visible: terminal-first onboarding, config files, high-confidence answers when fixing, and strong defaults without killing capability.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:09Z",
      "source_refs": ["Getting Started: \"all in about 5 minutes\"", "AGENTS.md: \"High-confidence answers only when fixing/triaging\"", "VISION.md: \"strong defaults without killing capability\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "craft.c.kill",
      "agent": "craft",
      "type": "kill",
      "parent": "craft.b",
      "content": "Build a fancier UI on top of Claude Code and call the polish the product.",
      "kill_reason": "Polish on closed plumbing is decoration, not craft. If prompts, tool contracts, and runtime behavior remain hidden, the UI only makes the black box easier to tolerate.",
      "killed_at": "2026-04-29T05:04:14Z",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:14Z",
      "source_refs": ["Tension: against overbuilt agent theater", "My Current AI Dev Workflow: \"Ghostty + Claude Code + minimal tooling = maximum productivity.\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "craft.c",
      "agent": "craft",
      "type": "revision",
      "parent": "craft.b",
      "content": "Make the craft inspectable: `AGENTS.md` as maintained operating memory, config schema as contract, skills that teach the agent, and plugin manifests instead of mystery behavior.",
      "supersedes": "craft.c.kill",
      "why_won": "The user can audit the assistant's working memory, tools, and defaults. That is the ergonomic move: confidence through inspectability.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:20Z",
      "source_refs": ["AGENTS.md", "Configuration", "Skills", "ClawHub: \"Equip Install Unleash.\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "craft.d",
      "agent": "craft",
      "type": "synthesis",
      "parent": "craft.c",
      "content": "Craft is not a shinier chat window. It is an assistant whose permissions, tools, memory, and failure modes are legible before they become dangerous.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:26Z",
      "source_refs": ["Security guide: \"Start with the smallest access that still works\"", "Security guide: \"access control before intelligence\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "infra.a",
      "agent": "infrastructure",
      "type": "prompt",
      "parent": null,
      "content": "Why is every team rebuilding the same agent loop primitives: tool calls, file edits, shell access, browser control, retries, and channel routing?",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:05Z",
      "source_refs": ["Tools and Plugins", "Gateway architecture: \"A single long-lived Gateway owns all messaging surfaces\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "infra.b",
      "agent": "infrastructure",
      "type": "critique",
      "parent": "infra.a",
      "content": "OpenClaw names the primitives: a Gateway owns messaging surfaces, a single embedded agent runtime runs the loop, and everything beyond text happens through tools.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:10Z",
      "source_refs": ["Gateway architecture", "Agent runtime: \"OpenClaw runs a single embedded agent runtime.\"", "Tools and Plugins"]
    },
    {
      "id": "infra.c.kill",
      "agent": "infrastructure",
      "type": "kill",
      "parent": "infra.b",
      "content": "Publish a clean spec and let everyone implement their own runner later.",
      "kill_reason": "Specs without a reference loop do not ship behavior. The hard semantics live in the runner: retries, tools, permissions, channel state, and safe defaults under pressure.",
      "killed_at": "2026-04-29T05:04:15Z",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:15Z",
      "source_refs": ["Agent runtime", "Configuration: \"If the file is missing, OpenClaw uses safe defaults.\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "infra.c",
      "agent": "infrastructure",
      "type": "revision",
      "parent": "infra.b",
      "content": "Ship the loop as infrastructure: Gateway, embedded runtime, plugin manifests, schema validation, allowlists, sandboxing, and tool profiles that make power explicit.",
      "supersedes": "infra.c.kill",
      "why_won": "A reference runner makes the contract executable. Teams can still compete on products, channels, and taste instead of re-implementing the same loop plumbing.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:21Z",
      "source_refs": ["Gateway architecture", "Configuration", "Security guide", "ClawHub"]
    },
    {
      "id": "infra.d",
      "agent": "infrastructure",
      "type": "synthesis",
      "parent": "infra.c",
      "content": "The loop is the infrastructure. Products should differentiate on judgment and experience, not on who wrote the least-bad shell wrapper.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:27Z",
      "source_refs": ["Tension: against one-off custom bots", "Tools and Plugins"]
    },
    {
      "id": "canon.a",
      "agent": "canon",
      "type": "prompt",
      "parent": null,
      "content": "What if Licklider, Engelbart, and Knowledge Navigator were waiting for a model that could operate real tools on a real computer?",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:06Z",
      "source_refs": ["Man-Computer Symbiosis", "Augmenting Human Intellect", "Knowledge Navigator"]
    },
    {
      "id": "canon.b",
      "agent": "canon",
      "type": "critique",
      "parent": "canon.a",
      "content": "A closed coding agent is a regression from small, composable tools. Peter's own workflow says to start with the model and a CLI first, because durable external handles beat bespoke UI wrappers.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:11Z",
      "source_refs": ["Shipping at Inference-Speed: \"Whatever you build, start with the model and a CLI first.\"", "Cross-reference: \"Pick services that have CLIs\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "canon.c.kill",
      "agent": "canon",
      "type": "kill",
      "parent": "canon.b",
      "content": "Rebuild it as one big AI-native monolithic agent.",
      "kill_reason": "That breaks the Unix lesson exactly where OpenClaw needs it most. A monolith hides composition, makes policy harder to audit, and turns tools back into a private ceremony.",
      "killed_at": "2026-04-29T05:04:16Z",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:16Z",
      "source_refs": ["Tension: against overbuilt agent theater", "VISION.md rejects \"Heavy orchestration layers\""]
    },
    {
      "id": "canon.c",
      "agent": "canon",
      "type": "revision",
      "parent": "canon.b",
      "content": "Keep it Unix-native: model plus CLI first, manifest-first control plane, tools as inspectable contracts, and a Gateway that lets the assistant run real tasks through ordinary channels.",
      "supersedes": "canon.c.kill",
      "why_won": "Composable interfaces let the operator swap, script, audit, and fork. That is why the old command-line lesson still matters for agents.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:22Z",
      "source_refs": ["AGENTS.md: \"manifest-first control plane\"", "Gateway architecture", "OpenClaw site"]
    },
    {
      "id": "canon.d",
      "agent": "canon",
      "type": "synthesis",
      "parent": "canon.c",
      "content": "The old personal-computing loop closes when the assistant becomes a scriptable operator, not another destination app.",
      "timestamp": "2026-04-29T05:04:28Z",
      "source_refs": ["Just Talk To It: \"Just talk to it.\"", "OpenClaw site: \"The AI that actually does things.\""]
    }
  ],
